# Detecting Kubernetes Secrets Dumps with Stratus Red Team and Sigma

## Why Bother?

Writing detection rules is easy. Writing detection rules that actually fire on real attack behavior, and don't drown you in false positives, is a different problem entirely. The classic mistake is writing a rule, deploying it, and just assuming it works. You never actually know until something fires, and by then it's too late to find out your rule had a typo in a field name for the last six months.

The answer is adversary simulation. You need to generate real, representative attack traffic against your own environment and confirm your detection pipeline catches it end to end. Not a unit test against fake log data, not a theoretical review of the rule logic, actual traffic, actual logs, actual alert.

That's what this post is about.

## What is Stratus Red Team?

[Stratus Red Team](https://github.com/DataDog/stratus-red-team) is an open source adversary emulation tool built by Datadog. Think of it as a library of self-contained attack techniques you can detonate against a live environment. Each technique maps to a MITRE ATT&CK tactic and handles its own setup and teardown, so you don't have to manually construct attack scenarios from scratch.

What makes it genuinely useful compared to just running the commands yourself is repeatability and precision. Every technique produces consistent, well-documented behavior. When you're trying to validate a detection rule, you want to know exactly what you fired and when, Stratus gives you that. It also tags all its traffic with a unique user agent, which is incredibly handy when you need to isolate simulated traffic from real activity in your logs.

It supports AWS, Azure, GCP, and Kubernetes, and the Kubernetes coverage is solid for the kinds of things you'd actually want to detect, credential access, lateral movement, persistence, privilege escalation.

## Simulating the Attack

To see what Kubernetes techniques are available, run:

bash

```bash
stratus list --platforms kubernetes
```

![[Pasted image 20260618100447.png]]

To get detail on a specific technique, use `stratus show`:

bash

```bash
stratus show k8s.credential-access.dump-secrets
```

![[Pasted image 20260618100552.png]]

You'll notice Stratus references both a warm-up and a detonation phase. Warm-up provisions any infrastructure the technique needs without executing the attack itself, things like creating a pod, setting up a service account with specific permissions, etc. Detonation is when the attack actually runs. This separation is useful because you can warm up, verify your environment is in the right state, and then detonate when you're ready.

For the secrets dump technique specifically, there's nothing to provision. It just targets whatever secrets already exist in your cluster, so warm-up is effectively a no-op. Detonate it directly:

bash

```bash
stratus detonate k8s.credential-access.dump-secrets
```

![[Pasted image 20260618100858.png]]

In my case it dumped 63 secrets from the cluster. That's every secret across every namespace in a single API call, exactly the kind of broad, noisy credential access behavior you want to be able to detect.

## Verifying the Raw Logs in Splunk

Before writing any detection logic, it's worth confirming the data actually landed and understanding what fields you're working with. Stratus tags all its traffic with a unique user agent, so we can use that to isolate the exact requests it made:

spl

```text
index=* sourcetype="kube:apiserver:audit" userAgent=stratus-red-team_ac5213f5-8f7e-41c9-92aa-d517b88e1eea
| where NOT match(requestURI, "namespaces")
| table _time user.username userAgent requestURI
```

![[Pasted image 20260618102637.png]]

This confirms the activity hit the k8s audit log and we can see exactly what fields are available. Get comfortable doing this step before you write rules, it tells you what the data actually looks like versus what you assume it looks like. Those two things are often different.

## The Detection Pipeline

Before getting into the rule, a quick note on how rules get deployed. I run a Sigma-to-Splunk CI/CD pipeline connected to GitHub, push a Sigma rule, it gets automatically converted to SPL and deployed to Splunk. It keeps everything version controlled and means I never have to manually touch the Splunk UI to manage rules.

The pipeline itself is fairly straightforward, but getting Kubernetes working took some extra effort. The standard pySigma Splunk backend doesn't have built-in support for Kubernetes log types, it knows about Windows, Linux, web servers, etc., but `product: k8s` isn't something it handles out of the box. The fix is a custom conversion script that manually injects the correct `sourcetype` and `index` at conversion time rather than relying on the default Sigma pipeline logic. Took a while to work out but once it's done it just works.

Here's the GitHub Actions workflow:

yaml

```yaml
name: Detection Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main
    paths:
      - 'detection/rules/**.yml'
      - 'detection/rules/**.yaml'
      - 'detection/pipelines/**.yaml'

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: self-hosted
    environment: production
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Generate savedsearches.conf
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          pip install pySigma-backend-splunk -q --break-system-packages
          python3 detection/scripts/convert_splunk.py | tee /tmp/savedsearches.conf
          cat /tmp/savedsearches.conf
          grep -v '^\[default\]' /tmp/savedsearches.conf | grep -q '^\[' || \
            { echo "ERROR: No rules converted"; exit 1; }

      - name: Deploy to Splunk pod
        env:
          SPLUNK_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.SPLUNK_PASSWORD }}
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          kubectl cp /tmp/savedsearches.conf \
            splunk/splunk-splunk-standalone-0:/opt/splunk/etc/apps/search/local/savedsearches.conf
          kubectl exec -n splunk splunk-splunk-standalone-0 -- \
            curl -sk -u admin:${SPLUNK_PASSWORD} \
            -X POST \
            https://localhost:8089/services/configs/conf-savedsearches/_reload
```

