The important design decision here is that `index` and `sourcetype` live in the pipeline config, not in the Sigma rules themselves. Sigma rules are supposed to be backend-agnostic — the same rule should convert cleanly to SPL, KQL, Lucene, or whatever else. Hardcoding `index=k8s-audit` into the rule breaks that. Injecting it at conversion time keeps the rules clean and portable.

## The Sigma Rule

yaml

```yaml
title: Kubernetes Cluster-Wide Secrets Dump
id: b2c3d4e5-f6a7-8901-bcde-f12345678901
status: experimental
description: Detects a cluster-wide dump of Kubernetes secrets via LIST /api/v1/secrets, characteristic of credential access attacks.
author: Austin Mundy
date: 2026-06-18
tags:
  - attack.credential_access
  - attack.t1552.007
logsource:
  product: k8s
  service: audit
detection:
  selection:
    verb: list
    objectRef.resource: secrets
    annotations.authorization.k8s.io/decision: allow
  filter_namespaced:
    requestURI|contains: namespaces
  filter_known_sa:
    user.username|startswith:
      - 'system:serviceaccount:'
      - 'system:kube'
      - 'system:k3s'
  condition: selection and not filter_namespaced and not filter_known_sa
falsepositives:
  - Legitimate cluster administrators auditing secrets
level: high
```

The rule targets `LIST` requests against `secrets` that were allowed by the Kubernetes authorizer. A couple of things worth explaining here since the filter logic matters.

`filter_namespaced` drops requests that contain `namespaces` in the URI. A namespaced secret dump, `GET /api/v1/namespaces/kube-system/secrets`, is a much more targeted operation and generates different noise levels than a true cluster-wide dump hitting `/api/v1/secrets` directly. We're specifically interested in the latter because it's the lazier, louder, more attacker-typical behavior.

`filter_known_sa` suppresses the system service accounts and Kubernetes internal components that legitimately list secrets as part of normal cluster operation. Without this filter the rule would be essentially unusable, there's a lot of internal Kubernetes traffic that looks exactly like this. k3s gets its own prefix filter because I'm running k3s and it has its own service account naming convention.

## Deploying and Verifying in Splunk

After pushing the rule to GitHub the pipeline converts it and deploys it automatically. You can find it in Splunk under Searches, Reports & Alerts by searching for the rule title.

![[Pasted image 20260618122205.png]]  
![[Pasted image 20260618124823.png]]

One limitation worth knowing about if you're running Splunk free tier: rules don't automatically become active alerts. In Splunk Enterprise Security, saved searches with detection logic get treated as correlation searches and run on a schedule automatically. On the free tier you need to open the saved search manually and configure it as a scheduled alert yourself. It's a bit clunky but totally workable for a homelab, just something to be aware of so you don't push a rule and assume it's alerting when it isn't.

Running the rule produces the following converted SPL:

spl

```text
index=k8s-audit sourcetype="kube:apiserver:audit" verb="list" objectRef.resource="secrets" "annotations.authorization.k8s.io/decision"="allow" NOT requestURI="*namespaces*" NOT (user.username IN ("system:serviceaccount:*", "system:kube*", "system:k3s*"))
```

And it returns 5 results from the simulated attack.

![[Pasted image 20260618125014.png]]

Rule fires, results match the Stratus detonation. Detection validated.

## Cleanup

Always run Stratus cleanup when you're done:

bash

```bash
stratus cleanup k8s.credential-access.dump-secrets
```

![[Pasted image 20260618125949.png]]

Since this technique didn't create any resources there's nothing to tear down here, but it's a good habit regardless. Some techniques create service accounts, RBAC bindings, pods, or other cluster resources during warm-up, and if you skip cleanup those stick around. Running cleanup every time means you never have to think about it.

---

## Wrapping Up

The full loop here, simulate, verify raw logs, write rule, deploy via pipeline, confirm it fires, is the right way to build detections you can actually trust. Stratus makes the simulation part dead simple, and once you have a Sigma pipeline set up the rest is just writing rules and pushing commits.

The Kubernetes-specific pipeline quirks are probably the biggest friction point if you're doing this yourself. The default pySigma tooling works great for standard log sources but you'll need the custom conversion script if your data doesn't fit the predefined log type mappings. Hopefully that part of this post saves someone a few hours.
